<Layout title="A young adult wellness program in Blacksburg | Kin & Campus" description="A unique young adult wellness program for the college years in Blacksburg. Membership with a board-certified pediatrician: chronic care, academic-calendar check-ins, and growing into adult health.">
<div class="w hero2"><div><div class="eyebrow">Blacksburg</div><h1>A year of growing into adult health.</h1><p class="lead">Kin &amp; Campus is a unique young adult wellness program you join for the college years in Blacksburg. A board-certified pediatrician who lives here holds that year with them: the medications that cannot lapse, the forms Virginia Tech asks for, the check-ins that follow the academic calendar, and the work of learning how to take care of themselves. Parents stay in the loop by agreement. The hometown doctor gets a note when the plan changes.</p><p class="hero-cta"><a class="btn" href={href("join")}>Join the program</a><a class="lnk" href={href("parents")}>For parents &rarr;</a><a class="lnk" href={href("students")}>For students &rarr;</a></p></div>
<img class="art" src={href("photos/drillfield-fall.jpg")} width="678" height="452" alt="Fall leaves on the Drillfield, Burruss Hall across the green">
</div>
<div class="w"><div class="trust"><div><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Chronic care that lasts</b><span>ADHD follow-up and refills, asthma, anxiety medication, allergies, acne. The plan continues through holidays and finals.</span></div><div><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><b>A year built around campus</b><span>Check-ins at move-in, after a break, around finals. Eight minutes from campus is how we see them in town.</span></div><div><svg class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><b>Family, by agreement</b><span>If they want you in the loop, we write that down and follow it. The hometown doctor still hears when the plan changes.</span></div></div></div>
<section><div class="w split"><div><div class="eyebrow">For parents</div><h2>You are enrolling them in a year.</h2><p>Families usually arrive with a list. ADHD or anxiety medication that cannot lapse. An inhaler. A sick day. A housing or sports form.</p><p>Inside the program those jobs sit next to something larger: a young adult learning how to take care of themselves with a real doctor, while you and the hometown physician stay named in the plan.</p><p><a class="lnk" href={href("parents")}>How enrollment works &rarr;</a></p></div>
<svg class="art" viewBox="0 0 600 190" role="img" aria-label="You, Kin and Campus, and your student, connected"><path d="M112 80Q190 10 265 66M335 66Q410 10 488 80" fill="none" stroke="#C9A24B" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="2 10" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="70" cy="82" r="42" fill="#6B2737"/><circle cx="70" cy="70" r="12" fill="#F6EFE4"/><path d="M46 104q24-26 48 0z" fill="#F6EFE4"/><circle cx="530" cy="82" r="42" fill="#C9A24B"/><circle cx="530" cy="70" r="12" fill="#2A1A1E"/><path d="M506 104q24-26 48 0z" fill="#2A1A1E"/><circle cx="300" cy="78" r="40" fill="#9CAF98"/><path d="M272 84L300 58 328 84v22H272z" fill="#FBF7F0" stroke="#6B2737" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="293" y="90" width="14" height="16" fill="#C9A24B"/><g font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle"><text x="70" y="150">You</text><text x="300" y="150">Kin &amp; Campus</text><text x="530" y="150">Your student</text></g></svg></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="w"><div class="eyebrow">While they are here</div><h2>What the year is made of.</h2>
<div class="notes">
<div class="note-row"><h3>Their plan</h3><p>Whatever they already take, we keep it going. Asthma, ADHD, anxiety medication, allergies, acne. We tell you if something changes, on the terms you agreed to together.</p></div>
<div class="note-row"><h3>A doctor who lives here</h3><p>A board-certified pediatrician in Blacksburg, close enough to see them when they need a room, and present enough to know them across a school year.</p></div>
<div class="note-row"><h3>How to take care of themselves</h3><p>College is when they start doing this without you. The program is where they learn appointments, refills, and when to ask for help, with a real doctor, rather than waiting until forty.</p></div>
<div class="note-row"><h3>Everyone on the same page</h3><p>Student, physician, parents, and the hometown doctor. We talk it through, then we write it down. Belonging includes being known in more than one place.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section><div class="w"><div class="eyebrow">The relationship</div><h2>A bridge to adult care. Parental guidance with student responsibility.</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><div><h3>The crossing</h3><p>These are the years when pediatric care is becoming adult care, and the student is still learning the adult parts. Kin &amp; Campus is built for that stretch in Blacksburg: a young adult wellness program, with a board-certified pediatrician who lives here, so the plan can last while they grow into holding it.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div><h3>What you still hold</h3><p>You enroll them. You help move the ADHD refill, the inhaler, the anxiety medication, the forms Virginia Tech will ask for. Together you decide what we may share. Guidance stays in the year; it simply stops being a job you do from two hundred miles away by guesswork.</p></div></li>
<li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><div><h3>What they begin to carry</h3><p>They book the visit. They ask for the refill before the bottle is empty. They learn when a fever is ordinary and when they need to be seen. At 18 the chart is theirs. Belonging to the program is how they practice that responsibility with a real doctor, while you remain named in the plan by agreement.</p></div></li>
</ol></div></section>
<section class="band"><div class="w"><div class="eyebrow">Privacy</div><h2>Privacy respected. Parents kept in the loop.</h2><p>At 18 the chart is theirs. Before they join, we write down what they want shared with you. We follow that. Their hometown doctor still gets a note the same day if the plan changes. <a class="lnk2" href={href("privacy")}>How sharing works &rarr;</a></p></div></section>
<section><div class="w"><h2>What belonging looks like.</h2><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>For your student</h3><p>A place in a small program in Blacksburg, and a pediatrician who treats them like an adult while they are still learning how.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>For you</h3><p>The knowledge that they belong to something while they are here, and that we will call you when you asked to be called.</p></div></div>
<p class="cite" style="max-width:640px;margin-top:28px">An ER visit can cost hundreds of dollars, or several thousand on a high-deductible plan. If it is a true emergency, call 911. Large-employer plans averaged $646 out of pocket. <a href="https://www.kff.org/health-costs/emergency-department-visits-exceed-affordability-threshold-for-many-consumers-with-private-insurance/">KFF / Peterson-Kaiser Health System Tracker</a>, 2019 claims (published 2022).</p>
<p style="margin-top:28px"><a class="btn" href={href("join")}>Join the program</a></p></div></section>
</Layout>
